The temptation will be to improve the protocol while porting it.
Don't. Port it verbatim first — same JSON field names, same filename
format (aq-{ts014d}-{ulid}.json), same severity logic. C-6 exists
specifically to catch drift. The Python prototype is the wire format
contract. Make the tests prove equivalence, then iterate.

../sb/main.go is the better pattern reference. cprr is 1700 lines
and has state machine complexity you don't need. sb is 960 lines
of "detect git state, format output, done" — that's what aq is.

The one thing not in the spec yet: aqHome() should check for .aq/
in cwd before falling back to ~/.aq/ — cprr does this with its
store and it matters for multi-repo isolation. The Python version
doesn't do this. It's the one deliberate improvement over the
prototype.

The ULID isn't a real ULID. It's a timestamp+random string.
Don't import a ULID library. Just match what the Python does:
12 hex digits of ms timestamp + 10 random lowercase alphanumeric.

Severity logic is three lines, not a table lookup:
  both proof + shared files = high
  one proof + shared files  = medium
  else + shared files       = low

Start with: types, ULID, storage paths, announce, readActive.
Then conflict detection. Then CLI dispatch. Tests last.
